# hangar configuration.
#
# This file is the ONLY place hangar reads settings from. It deliberately does
# not fall back to your shell environment, so a fleet behaves identically no
# matter what terminal, service manager, or cron job started it.
#
# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored.

# A GitHub token that can manage this org's runners; without it the short-lived
# registration and removal tokens cannot be minted.
#
# Create a fine-grained token whose resource owner is the ORG (not your personal
# account), with the organization permission "Self-hosted runners" set to
# "Read and write". No repository permissions are needed.
#
# Check it with `make status` before scaling anything. See README, "Getting a token".
GH_TOKEN=

# The organization whose runners these are.
GH_ORG=

# Runner group to register into. Leave blank for the org's default group.
# Groups require GitHub Team or Enterprise.
RUNNER_GROUP=

# Extra labels, comma separated. Every worker already carries self-hosted plus
# its OS and architecture from the runner itself (macOS and ARM64 on an Apple
# Silicon Mac, Linux and X64 on a PC), plus `hangar` from hangar — target that
# one to reach this fleet rather than any other self-hosted machine in the org:
#
#     runs-on: [self-hosted, hangar]
#
# Leave this blank unless a workflow needs something more specific still, like
# a machine with a particular SDK. Anything set here is added to those labels,
# never a replacement for them.
RUNNER_LABELS=

# Worker N registers as <prefix>N. Defaults to <hostname>-w.
RUNNER_NAME_PREFIX=

# The docker socket every worker should talk to. Leave blank and hangar finds
# it automatically: Docker Desktop's ~/.docker/run/docker.sock when that
# exists, then rootless Docker's /run/user/<uid>/docker.sock, then the system
# daemon's /var/run/docker.sock.
#
# It has to be stated at all because each worker runs with a private HOME, which
# has no docker contexts to resolve the daemon from — and Docker Desktop never
# creates /var/run/docker.sock. Set this only to point at a different daemon.
# Example: unix:///Users/you/.docker/run/docker.sock
#
# `none` says jobs get no docker daemon by design, say because every build goes to
# a buildx remote builder: hangar then looks for no socket, hands none to the
# workers, and does not ask for the docker group before it scales.
DOCKER_HOST=

# Paths, relative to your real home, to share back into every worker instead of
# isolating. Isolation is the default: each worker gets its own HOME, so every
# tool that keeps state in a dotfile — docker, gcloud, aws, npm, kubectl, ssh,
# git, cargo — is separated without being named here.
#
# Share only things where isolation costs something and protects nothing, which
# in practice means caches. Anything holding credentials should stay isolated.
# Example: SHARE_PATHS=.npm,.cache/go-build
SHARE_PATHS=

# PATH that a worker's jobs run with. Written out rather than inherited so a
# job's toolchain never silently depends on whichever shell ran `make`. Leave
# blank for the platform's standard set: on macOS the system directories plus
# Homebrew and ~/.docker/bin, on Linux the system directories plus /snap/bin.
RUNNER_PATH=

# Locale for jobs. Leave blank for en_US.UTF-8 on macOS and C.UTF-8 on Linux,
# the one UTF-8 locale a minimal Linux install is guaranteed to have.
RUNNER_LANG=

# Where each worker's TMPDIR lives, as <WORKER_TMP_ROOT>/w<N>. Leave blank to
# keep it inside the worker directory. Point it at a size-capped tmpfs or RAM
# disk to keep build scratch files off the disk; each worker recreates its
# directory before every start, so one wiped by a reboot is fine. Must be an
# absolute path to a directory this account owns, where neither it nor
# anything it sits inside is a directory every account can write to (/tmp,
# /var/tmp, /dev/shm, or a tmpfs mounted with a permissive mode) — mount it,
# for example, with mode=0700,uid=<fleet account>. A worker refuses to start
# unless both the root and its w<N> hold to that. hangar deletes
# <WORKER_TMP_ROOT>/w<N> when the worker goes.
# Example: WORKER_TMP_ROOT=/mnt/hangar-tmp
WORKER_TMP_ROOT=

# Where `make metrics` serves Prometheus metrics: workers up and busy, the job
# each is running (name, workflow, repository, run id), jobs finished by result,
# and job durations. Loopback only by default — the metrics name repositories
# and jobs; widen it only behind something that restricts who can scrape.
# Loopback is not private from other accounts on this machine; see SECURITY.md.
# Default: 127.0.0.1:9151
METRICS_ADDR=

# How long a job may run on a worker, in minutes, before hangar stops it and
# fails it. 0 means no limit — GitHub's own six-hour default then applies.
# Default: 30. Up to 7200, GitHub's five-day ceiling for self-hosted jobs.
#
# At the limit hangar stops the step that is running, as if it had been killed:
# the job fails, and its post steps, container clean-up and `if: failure()`
# steps still run, with five minutes to finish before the runner is stopped
# outright. The job's annotations say why it failed. A job's own
# `timeout-minutes` still applies, whichever comes first.
#
# Read when each job starts, so a new value takes effect with the next job.
# Workers provisioned by a hangar from before this setting need their files
# rewritten once — `make <N>` does that for every idle worker.
JOB_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=
